<BlogPost title="Building CourtVision: A 26-Week Solo Journey" date="2026-07-01">
  <p>
    Twenty-six weeks. That's how long it's been since I started building CourtVision — a basketball analytics platform designed to help players and coaches make data-driven decisions.
  </p>

  <h2>The Idea</h2>
  <p>
    It started with a simple observation: basketball data is everywhere, but actionable insights are rare. I wanted to build something that would bridge that gap — a tool that could turn raw stats into meaningful narratives.
  </p>

  <h2>The Stack</h2>
  <p>
    I chose a modern stack that I was comfortable with but also wanted to push my boundaries:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Frontend:</strong> React with TypeScript</li>
    <li><strong>Backend:</strong> Node.js with Express</li>
    <li><strong>Database:</strong> PostgreSQL</li>
    <li><strong>Visualization:</strong> D3.js</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The Lessons</h2>
  <p>
    Building solo is a different kind of challenge. There's no one to bounce ideas off of, no code reviews, no standup meetings. You have to be your own product manager, designer, and developer.
  </p>

  <p>
    The biggest lesson? <strong>Ship early, iterate often.</strong> I spent the first two weeks perfecting the database schema before writing a single line of frontend code. In hindsight, I should have started with a prototype and iterated from there.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    "Perfect is the enemy of shipped." — Every developer who's ever over-engineered something
  </blockquote>

  <h2>What's Next</h2>
  <p>
    CourtVision is still a work in progress, but I'm proud of how far it's come. The next phase involves integrating real-time game data and building out the coaching dashboard.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you're interested in following the journey, check out the <a href="https://github.com/Erioluwa-dev/CourtVision">GitHub repo</a> or reach out — I'd love to hear your thoughts.
  </p>
</BlogPost>
